              "text": "{\n  \"query_text\": \"Under the WHO guidelines aiming to simultaneously reduce sodium and increase potassium, what is the resulting molar ratio of sodium to potassium if both guidelines are achieved, what are the recommended primary food sources to increase potassium, and which specific health conditions or treatments exclude adults from the general potassium recommendation?\",\n  \"target_population\": [\n    \"Adults in general populations\",\n    \"Children\"\n  ],\n  \"conditions\": [\n    \"Kidney impairments\",\n    \"Conditions compromising potassium excretion\",\n    \"Hypertension\"\n  ],\n  \"draft_rationale\": \"To answer this query, information must be integrated from both passages. The 2012 guideline (Retained) establishes the policy of simultaneously reducing sodium and increasing potassium, stating that achieving both guidelines results in a sodium-to-potassium molar ratio of approximately 1:1. The 2025 guideline (Current) specifies the primary food sources for potassium (beans, peas, nuts, and green vegetables) and details the exclusions for the general adult population, which include individuals with kidney impairments, those with conditions compromising potassium excretion, and those taking potassium-sparing diuretics or potassium supplements.\"\n}",
